.TH SAPHIRA-MERGETOOL 1 "September 2026" "saphira-mergetool 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
saphira-mergetool \- resolve apk .apk-new upgrade artifacts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B saphira-mergetool
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B saphira-mergetool --diff
.RI [ FILE ]
.br
.B saphira-mergetool --accept FILE|all
.br
.B saphira-mergetool --keep FILE|all
.br
.B saphira-mergetool --merge FILE
.br
.B saphira-mergetool --auto
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
When apk upgrades a
.B protected
file (everything under
.BR /etc ,
plus any tree covered by
.BR /etc/apk/protected_paths.d )
and the installed copy was locally modified, the new package version
is not applied automatically. It lands beside the installed original
as
.IR FILE .apk-new
and waits for the operator to decide. Nothing is merged silently.
.PP
.B saphira-mergetool
makes that decision easy. It scans configured roots for pending
.BR .apk-new
files, shows unified diffs against the installed originals, and
resolves each pair one of three ways:
.TP
.B accept
The
.B .apk-new
version replaces the installed file. An orphan
.B .apk-new
whose original is gone is promoted to the real name.
.TP
.B keep
The installed file stays exactly as it is; the
.B .apk-new
is discarded.
.TP
.B merge
Interactive, hunk by hunk:
.BR [y]es ,
.BR [n]o ,
.B all-remaining ,
.BR [q]uit .
Local edits survive in every declined hunk; mode and ownership follow
the installed file, written atomically (temporary file, mode and
ownership copied, rename into place).
.PP
With no action flags the tool only lists what is pending (flagging
identical and orphan files). Every action also runs under
.BR --dry-run .
.SH WHEN TO MERGE, ACCEPT, OR KEEP
Always read the diff first
.RB ( --diff ).
The right action depends on what the diff shows, not on habit.
.TP
.B Merge
when both sides carry value: you have local edits worth preserving
.I and
upstream changed something you want (new options, fixed defaults,
security-relevant behavior). Typical candidates are
.B /etc/conf.d
service configurations and init scripts with local tweaks.
.TP
.B Accept
when the installed copy holds nothing worth keeping: the diff is
empty or trivial, the file is package-owned data you never edited,
or the
.B .apk-new
is an orphan whose original disappeared. Accepting an orphan
promotes it to the real name, which is the correct outcome.
.TP
.B Keep
when the installed file deliberately diverges and the upstream
changes are unwanted or inapplicable to this machine.
.SH WHEN NOT TO MERGE
.TP
.B Never merge blind.
If you have not read the diff, you have not made a decision.
Use
.B --diff
first, and
.B --dry-run
before any write.
.TP
.B Never --accept all on first sight.
List first, review each file, then resolve deliberately or encode
routine paths in the
.B [policy]
section and use
.BR --auto .
.TP
.B Never accept passwd, group or shadow wholesale.
Upstream account databases routinely gain new service rows (for
example a new
.B proxyto
user and group). Accepting the package version would wipe every
locally created user. Instead diff the pair, extract only the new
upstream rows, and append those rows to the installed files by
hand (checking
.BR vipw (8)
conventions: unique names, unique numeric IDs, valid shells).
Then discard the
.BR .apk-new .
.TP
.B Never hunk-merge binary or non-text files.
The differ is byte-oriented; merging a binary hunk by hunk
corrupts it. For non-text files choose whole-file
.B accept
or
.B keep
only.
.TP
.B Never merge what you do not understand.
If a diff is unclear, do nothing: an unresolved
.B .apk-new
sits harmlessly beside the installed file. Aborting a merge with
.B q
likewise writes nothing and keeps the
.B .apk-new
for later. Research first, resolve second.
.TP
.B Never merge symlinks by content.
Ownership and link targets are handled as links, not text; use
.B accept
or
.B keep
for the whole link.
.SH POLICY FILE
.B /etc/saphira/mergetool.conf
(CLI flags override it):
.PP
.RS
.nf
[mergetool]
scan_paths = /etc /srv/gopher-source

[policy]
# fnmatch globs against the installed path or basename
/etc/init.d/* = accept
/etc/conf.d/* = keep
.fi
.RE
.PP
With
.BR --auto ,
configured policies apply non-interactively; unmatched files are
prompted for on a terminal, or reported as pending in scripts.
.SH EXAMPLES
Typical session after an upgrade:
.PP
.RS
.nf
saphira-mergetool
saphira-mergetool --diff
saphira-mergetool --merge /etc/conf.d/saphira-build.apk-new
saphira-mergetool --accept /etc/init.d/saphira-build.apk-new
.fi
.RE
.PP
Absorbing a new service account (upstream added
.BR proxyto ,
local users must survive):
.PP
.RS
.nf
saphira-mergetool --diff /etc/passwd.apk-new
# confirm the only additions are the proxyto rows,
# append those two lines to /etc/passwd (and likewise
# group, shadow), then discard the artifact:
saphira-mergetool --keep /etc/passwd.apk-new
.fi
.RE
.PP
Routine non-interactive pass:
.PP
.RS
.nf
saphira-mergetool --auto --dry-run
saphira-mergetool --auto
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/saphira/mergetool.conf
Operator configuration: scan roots and resolution policy.
.TP
.B /etc/apk/protected_paths.d
Additional trees apk protects (and therefore produces
.B .apk-new
files under).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or nothing pending. Dry runs always exit 0.
.TP
.B 1
A merge was aborted, a scan path is not a directory, the
configuration is unreadable, or a file is not an
.B .apk-new
artifact. Aborts write nothing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apk (8),
.BR vipw (8)
